So `free_context` frees all `desc[i].buf` for `i < max_tok*16`. The double-free would occur if two `desc[i].buf` pointers point to the same allocation. Let me look at the copy path again:

```c
i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15);
if (j >= i)
    goto err;

ctx->desc[i].buf_l = 0;
ctx->desc[i].buf_a = ctx->desc[j].buf_a;
if (ctx->desc[i].buf) free(ctx->desc[i].buf);
ctx->desc[i].buf = malloc(ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
if (!ctx->desc[i].buf)
    goto err;

memcpy(ctx->desc[i].buf, ctx->desc[j].buf, ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
```

This makes a copy, so no aliasing. But wait - what about the case where `ctx->desc[j].buf` is NULL? `memcpy` with NULL source would crash but not double-free.

